// Windows filesystem-watch primitives — the third backend the header of
// `backend.cplus` predicted. Replaces it through C+'s platform-override
// convention, the same way `backend_linux.cplus` does.
//
// ---- THE SHAPE IS NOT kqueue's OR inotify's ----------------------------------
//
// Both of those hand out a QUEUE descriptor and let you register watches
// against it, which is why the seam is `queue_open` / `watch_path` /
// `poll_one`. Windows has no queue: `ReadDirectoryChangesW` is issued PER
// DIRECTORY HANDLE, and its result arrives through an OVERLAPPED structure
// with an event the caller waits on. So the queue is synthesised here — a table
// of watch slots tagged with a queue id — and `poll_one` walks the slots for
// one queue asking each event whether it is signalled, with a zero timeout.
//
// The seam survives that because it only ever carries an `i32` opaque handle:
// on kqueue it is an `O_EVTONLY` fd, on inotify a watch descriptor, and here it
// is an index into the table below. Nothing outside this file interprets it.
//
// ---- MTIME HERE IS COARSE, AND THE USN IS WHY THAT NO LONGER BOUNDS US ------
//
// The engine tells one version of a file from another by (inode, size, mtime).
// Windows stamps `LastWriteTime` from the system clock, which advances about
// every 13ms — the FILETIME has 100ns UNITS but nothing like 100ns RESOLUTION —
// so two writes of the same number of bytes inside one tick produce three
// identical fields and are indistinguishable. Measured on this host, rewriting
// a 1-byte file with no gap:
//
//     before  size=1 mtime=1788879411.288213800
//     after   size=1 mtime=1788879411.288213800   <- the same, byte for byte
//     with a 1ms gap: .289201200 -> .302375600    <- one tick apart
//
// Darwin and Linux stamp from a high-resolution clock and do not collide, so
// this is the one place the three backends genuinely differ in what the
// PLATFORM can report.
//
// SO `Metadata` CARRIES A FOURTH FIELD, `version`, and this backend fills it
// from the NTFS USN — a per-file counter that moves on every change. See
// `_usn_of`. Darwin and Linux answer a constant 0, which compares equal every
// time and so changes nothing for them.
//
// AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS HEADER SAID THE FIX WAS NOT WORTH IT, on the
// grounds that it would widen the backend contract. That was wrong twice over
// and is worth recording, because both errors are the kind that stop a cheap
// fix from being attempted. It does not touch the seam at all —
// `queue_open`/`watch_path`/`poll_one` are untouched; `metadata` is already a
// per-platform function and the differ already reads only what it returns. And
// it costs no extra file open: `metadata` opens a handle for
// `GetFileInformationByHandle` anyway, so the USN is one `DeviceIoControl` on a
// handle that is already in hand.
//
// The other road — keeping the `FILE_NOTIFY_INFORMATION` records and telling
// the engine WHICH path changed — WOULD widen the seam, and is still not taken.
// It is also no longer needed for this.
//
// In practice a caller was usually past the tick by construction anyway:
// `Watcher::run` polls every 50ms. It is a program writing twice in a row with
// nothing in between that lands inside one — and that is now seen too.

// ---- EVENTS ARE A WAKEUP, NOT A PAYLOAD -------------------------------------
//
// `poll_one` reports only 1/0/-1/-2, never WHAT changed, because the engine
// above is a snapshot differ: it rescans and compares. That is what makes the
// Windows port tractable — the `FILE_NOTIFY_INFORMATION` records
// `ReadDirectoryChangesW` writes are read and discarded, and the buffer exists
// only because the call requires one. It also means a buffer overflow is not a
// correctness problem: the rescan finds everything regardless.
//
// Watches are per-directory and NOT recursive (`bWatchSubtree` is false), which
// matches the other two backends — the engine registers a watch for every
// directory it walks and expects exactly one wakeup source per directory.
//
// ---- `metadata` IS AN lstat ---------------------------------------------------
//
// `FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT` is what makes it one: without it the open
// follows a symlink and reports the target, and the snapshot engine explicitly
// does not want that. `GetFileInformationByHandle` then answers attributes,
// size, mtime AND the file index in one call — and the index matters, because
// the engine uses `inode` to match a deleted entry against a created one when
// it detects renames. A backend that returned 0 for every inode would make
// every deletion look like a rename of the first candidate.

// ---- THE USN, which is what makes a same-tick rewrite visible ----------------
//
// The limitation above is real and this is the fix for it. NTFS keeps a per-file
// Update Sequence Number that moves on EVERY change, and `_usn_of` reads it on
// the handle `metadata` has already opened — so it costs one `DeviceIoControl`
// and no extra open. Measured on this host against the exact failure: two
// same-size writes back to back left `LastWriteTime` byte-identical
// (134334427044088330 both times) while the USN moved 22844915344 ->
// 22844915608. The first is why the engine missed the second write; the second
// is why it no longer does.
//
// `FSCTL_READ_FILE_USN_DATA` NEEDS NO MORE ACCESS than the
// `FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES` this file already asks for, which was the thing worth
// checking before building any of it — a fix that needed a read handle would
// have failed on every file another process holds exclusively.
//
// ZERO ON ANY REFUSAL, and that is the whole error policy. A non-NTFS volume
// (FAT32, exFAT, a network share) answers ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION, and a volume
// whose journal is off answers zeroes. Both come back as 0, which compares
// equal every time and so restores exactly the old mtime-only behaviour rather
// than reporting spurious changes. A watcher that cried wolf on a USB stick
// would be worse than one that keeps the 13ms blind spot there.

import "stdlib/text" as text;
import "stdlib/option" as option;
// The tests below write real files on a real volume — see their header for why
// nothing else would exercise a property of NTFS.
import "stdlib/fs" as fs;
import "stdlib/vec" as vec;
import "stdlib/result" as result;

extern fn CreateFileW(path: *u8, access: u32, share: u32, sa: *u8,
                      disposition: u32, flags: u32, template: *u8) -> *u8;
extern fn CloseHandle(h: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn GetFileInformationByHandle(h: *u8, info: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn GetLastError() -> u32;
extern fn CreateEventW(sa: *u8, manual_reset: i32, initial: i32, name: *u8) -> *u8;
extern fn ResetEvent(h: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn WaitForSingleObject(h: *u8, ms: u32) -> u32;
extern fn ReadDirectoryChangesW(dir: *u8, buf: *u8, len: u32, subtree: i32,
                                filter: u32, returned: *u32, ovl: *u8,
                                completion: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn GetOverlappedResult(h: *u8, ovl: *u8, transferred: *u32, wait: i32) -> i32;
extern fn CancelIo(h: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn DeviceIoControl(h: *u8, code: u32, in_buf: *u8, in_len: u32,
                          out_buf: *u8, out_len: u32, returned: *u32, ovl: *u8) -> i32;
extern fn MultiByteToWideChar(codepage: u32, flags: u32, mb: *u8, mb_len: i32,
                              wide: *u8, wide_len: i32) -> i32;
extern fn malloc(n: usize) -> *u8;
extern fn free(p: *u8);
extern fn memset(p: *u8, v: i32, n: usize) -> *u8;

// ---- constants, transcribed from the SDK headers on this host ----------------

fn _cp_utf8() -> u32 { return 65001 as u32; }
fn _nul_terminated() -> i32 { return 0 - 1; }

fn _file_read_attributes() -> u32 { return 0x80 as u32; }
fn _file_list_directory() -> u32 { return 0x1 as u32; }
fn _share_all() -> u32 { return 0x7 as u32; }          // READ|WRITE|DELETE
fn _open_existing() -> u32 { return 3 as u32; }
fn _flag_backup_semantics() -> u32 { return 0x02000000 as u32; }
fn _flag_open_reparse_point() -> u32 { return 0x00200000 as u32; }
fn _flag_overlapped() -> u32 { return 0x40000000 as u32; }

fn _attr_directory() -> u32 { return 0x10 as u32; }
fn _attr_reparse_point() -> u32 { return 0x400 as u32; }

// FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_{FILE_NAME,DIR_NAME,ATTRIBUTES,SIZE,LAST_WRITE,CREATION}.
fn _notify_filter() -> u32 { return 0x5F as u32; }

fn _wait_object_0() -> u32 { return 0 as u32; }

fn _err_file_not_found() -> i32 { return 2 as i32; }
fn _err_path_not_found() -> i32 { return 3 as i32; }
fn _err_access_denied() -> i32 { return 5 as i32; }

// INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE is (HANDLE)-1, not null — the one Win32 sentinel that
// catches everyone, since every other handle-returning call uses null.
fn _invalid_handle() -> *u8 { return { ((0 as usize) -% (1 as usize)) as *u8 }; }

fn _is_bad_handle(h: *u8) -> bool {
    return h == (0 as *u8) || h == _invalid_handle();
}

// BY_HANDLE_FILE_INFORMATION: 52 bytes. Offsets verified with `offsetof`
// against the real header, not read off a manual page.
fn _bhfi_bytes() -> usize { return 52 as usize; }
fn _off_attrs() -> usize { return 0 as usize; }
fn _off_last_write() -> usize { return 20 as usize; }
fn _off_size_high() -> usize { return 32 as usize; }
fn _off_size_low() -> usize { return 36 as usize; }
fn _off_index_high() -> usize { return 44 as usize; }
fn _off_index_low() -> usize { return 48 as usize; }

// OVERLAPPED is 32 bytes with hEvent at 24.
fn _overlapped_bytes() -> usize { return 32 as usize; }
fn _off_hevent() -> usize { return 24 as usize; }

fn _notify_buf_bytes() -> usize { return 8192 as usize; }

fn _read_u32(p: *u8, at: usize) -> u32 {
    return { *({ ({ #addr(p) } +% at) as *u32 }) };
}

// ---- the public types, identical to backend.cplus ----------------------------

enum NodeKind {
    File,
    Directory,
    Symlink,
    Other,
}

struct Metadata {
    kind: NodeKind,
    inode: u64,
    size: i64,
    mtime_sec: i64,
    mtime_nsec: i64,
    mode: u16,
    // THE FIELD THIS BACKEND EXISTS TO FILL. See `backend.cplus` for the
    // contract; `_usn_of` below for what goes in it and when it cannot.
    version: u64,
}

enum MetadataResult {
    Found(Metadata),
    Missing,
    PermissionDenied,
    InvalidPath,
    Failed,
}

fn errno_value() -> i32 {
    return { GetLastError() } as i32;
}

// UTF-8 path to a malloc'd UTF-16 buffer the caller frees, or null.
fn _widen(path: str) -> *u8 {
    let owned: text::Text = text::from_str(path);
    match owned.c_str() {
        option::Option[text::CString]::Some(cp) => {
            let need: i32 = { MultiByteToWideChar(_cp_utf8(), 0 as u32, cp.as_ptr(),
                                                  _nul_terminated(), 0 as *u8, 0 as i32) };
            if need <= (0 as i32) { return 0 as *u8; }
            let wide: *u8 = { malloc((need as usize) *% (2 as usize)) };
            if wide == (0 as *u8) { return 0 as *u8; }
            let got: i32 = { MultiByteToWideChar(_cp_utf8(), 0 as u32, cp.as_ptr(),
                                                 _nul_terminated(), wide, need) };
            if got <= (0 as i32) { free(wide); return 0 as *u8; }
            return wide;
        }
        option::Option[text::CString]::None => { return 0 as *u8; }
    }
}

// FILETIME counts 100 ns intervals since 1601-01-01; the Unix epoch is
// 116444736000000000 of them later.
fn _epoch_delta_100ns() -> u64 { return 116444736000000000 as u64; }

fn _filetime_at(info: *u8, at: usize) -> u64 {
    let lo: u32 = _read_u32(info, at);
    let hi: u32 = _read_u32(info, at +% (4 as usize));
    return ((hi as u64) *% (4294967296 as u64)) +% (lo as u64);
}

// FSCTL_READ_FILE_USN_DATA. The control code is
// CTL_CODE(FILE_DEVICE_FILE_SYSTEM(9), 58, METHOD_NEITHER(3), FILE_ANY_ACCESS(0))
// = (9 << 16) | (58 << 2) | 3 = 0x900EB, folded here rather than written as a
// bare number so it can be checked against the macro.
fn _fsctl_read_file_usn_data() -> u32 {
    return ((9 as u32) *% (65536 as u32)) +% ((58 as u32) *% (4 as u32)) +% (3 as u32);
}

// USN_RECORD_V2 puts the number at offset 24: RecordLength(4) MajorVersion(2)
// MinorVersion(2) FileReferenceNumber(8) ParentFileReferenceNumber(8), then Usn.
fn _off_usn() -> usize { return 24 as usize; }
fn _usn_record_bytes() -> usize { return 320 as usize; }

// The file's change counter on an already-open handle, or 0 when this volume
// has none. Never fails outward: 0 is a legal answer meaning "no tie-breaker".
fn _usn_of(h: *u8) -> u64 {
    let buf: *u8 = { malloc(_usn_record_bytes()) };
    if buf == (0 as *u8) { return 0 as u64; }
    { memset(buf, 0 as i32, _usn_record_bytes()) };
    var returned: u32 = 0 as u32;
    let ok: i32 = { DeviceIoControl(h, _fsctl_read_file_usn_data(), 0 as *u8, 0 as u32,
                                    buf, _usn_record_bytes() as u32,
                                    #addr_of(returned), 0 as *u8) };
    // A SHORT ANSWER IS AS BAD AS A REFUSAL. The record is variable-length and a
    // reply that stopped before the Usn field would leave this reading zeroed
    // buffer as though it were a real number — which is harmless (0 disables the
    // tie-break) but only because the buffer was cleared first.
    if ok == (0 as i32) { free(buf); return 0 as u64; }
    if (returned as usize) < (_off_usn() +% (8 as usize)) { free(buf); return 0 as u64; }
    let lo: u32 = _read_u32(buf, _off_usn());
    let hi: u32 = _read_u32(buf, _off_usn() +% (4 as usize));
    free(buf);
    return ((hi as u64) *% (4294967296 as u64)) +% (lo as u64);
}

fn metadata(path: str) -> MetadataResult {
    let wide: *u8 = _widen(path);
    if wide == (0 as *u8) { return MetadataResult::InvalidPath; }
    // OPEN_REPARSE_POINT is what makes this an lstat; BACKUP_SEMANTICS is what
    // lets a DIRECTORY be opened at all.
    let h: *u8 = { CreateFileW(wide, _file_read_attributes(), _share_all(),
                               0 as *u8, _open_existing(),
                               _flag_backup_semantics() | _flag_open_reparse_point(),
                               0 as *u8) };
    // READ THE ERROR BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. `GetLastError` is per-thread and
    // clobbered by the NEXT api call that sets it — `free` reaches HeapFree and
    // does. Capturing it after the cleanup reported whatever the allocator last
    // thought, which showed up as a plain file coming back PermissionDenied.
    let err: i32 = errno_value();
    free(wide);
    if _is_bad_handle(h) {
        let e: i32 = err;
        if e == _err_file_not_found() { return MetadataResult::Missing; }
        if e == _err_path_not_found() { return MetadataResult::Missing; }
        if e == _err_access_denied() { return MetadataResult::PermissionDenied; }
        return MetadataResult::Failed;
    }
    let info: *u8 = { malloc(_bhfi_bytes()) };
    if info == (0 as *u8) { let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(h) }; return MetadataResult::Failed; }
    let ok: i32 = { GetFileInformationByHandle(h, info) };
    // WHILE THE HANDLE IS STILL OPEN. This is the whole cost of the tie-break:
    // one ioctl on a handle that had to be opened anyway, and no second open.
    let version: u64 = _usn_of(h);
    let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(h) };
    if ok == (0 as i32) { free(info); return MetadataResult::Failed; }

    let attrs: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_attrs());
    var kind: NodeKind = NodeKind::File;
    var mode: u16 = 0x8000 as u16;                       // S_IFREG
    if (attrs & _attr_reparse_point()) != (0 as u32) {
        kind = NodeKind::Symlink;
        mode = 0xA000 as u16;                            // S_IFLNK
    } else {
        if (attrs & _attr_directory()) != (0 as u32) {
            kind = NodeKind::Directory;
            mode = 0x4000 as u16;                        // S_IFDIR
        }
    }

    let size_hi: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_size_high());
    let size_lo: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_size_low());
    let size: i64 = (((size_hi as u64) *% (4294967296 as u64)) +% (size_lo as u64)) as i64;

    let idx_hi: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_index_high());
    let idx_lo: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_index_low());
    let inode: u64 = ((idx_hi as u64) *% (4294967296 as u64)) +% (idx_lo as u64);

    let ft: u64 = _filetime_at(info, _off_last_write());
    var msec: i64 = 0 as i64;
    var mnsec: i64 = 0 as i64;
    if ft >= _epoch_delta_100ns() {
        let since: u64 = ft -% _epoch_delta_100ns();
        msec = (since / (10000000 as u64)) as i64;
        mnsec = ((since % (10000000 as u64)) *% (100 as u64)) as i64;
    }
    free(info);

    return MetadataResult::Found(Metadata {
        kind: kind,
        inode: inode,
        size: size,
        mtime_sec: msec,
        mtime_nsec: mnsec,
        mode: mode,
        version: version,
    });
}

// ---- the synthesised queue ---------------------------------------------------
//
// One flat table. `queue` is 0 for a free slot, so queue ids start at 1 and
// `queue_open` never hands back an id that `#zero` could be mistaken for.

const MAX_WATCHES: usize = 256;
const MAX_QUEUES: i32 = 32;

struct WatchSlots {
    queue: [i32; 256],
    dir: [usize; 256],
    ev: [usize; 256],
    ovl: [usize; 256],
    buf: [usize; 256],
    // WHICH DIRECTORY THIS SLOT IS ACTUALLY WATCHING, by identity rather than by
    // path — volume serial plus file index, which is Windows' inode and is what
    // `\tmp` and `C:\tmp\.` agree on. It exists so a second target under a
    // directory already being watched can take a passive slot instead of opening
    // a duplicate handle; see `watch_path`. 0/0 on a passive slot.
    vol: [u32; 256],
    ident: [usize; 256],
}

static SLOTS: WatchSlots = #zero::[WatchSlots]();
static NEXT_QUEUE: i32 = 0;

fn queue_open() -> i32 {
    if NEXT_QUEUE >= MAX_QUEUES { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    NEXT_QUEUE = NEXT_QUEUE + (1 as i32);
    return NEXT_QUEUE;
}

fn _free_slot() -> isize {
    var i: usize = 0 as usize;
    while i < MAX_WATCHES {
        if SLOTS.queue[i] == (0 as i32) { return i as isize; }
        i = i +% (1 as usize);
    }
    return (0 as isize) -% (1 as isize);
}

fn _arm(idx: usize) -> bool {
    let dir: *u8 = { SLOTS.dir[idx] as *u8 };
    let buf: *u8 = { SLOTS.buf[idx] as *u8 };
    let ovl: *u8 = { SLOTS.ovl[idx] as *u8 };
    // The OVERLAPPED must be cleared before every reissue — a stale Internal
    // field makes the call report the PREVIOUS result.
    let ev: usize = SLOTS.ev[idx];
    { memset(ovl, 0 as i32, _overlapped_bytes()) };
    { *({ ({ #addr(ovl) } +% _off_hevent()) as **u8 }) = { ev as *u8 } };
    let rc: i32 = { ReadDirectoryChangesW(dir, buf, _notify_buf_bytes() as u32,
                                          0 as i32, _notify_filter(),
                                          0 as *u32, ovl, 0 as *u8) };
    return rc != (0 as i32);
}

fn _release(idx: usize) {
    let dir: *u8 = { SLOTS.dir[idx] as *u8 };
    if !_is_bad_handle(dir) {
        let _x: i32 = { CancelIo(dir) };
        let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(dir) };
    }
    let ev: *u8 = { SLOTS.ev[idx] as *u8 };
    if !_is_bad_handle(ev) { let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(ev) }; }
    let ovl: *u8 = { SLOTS.ovl[idx] as *u8 };
    if ovl != (0 as *u8) { free(ovl); }
    let buf: *u8 = { SLOTS.buf[idx] as *u8 };
    if buf != (0 as *u8) { free(buf); }
    SLOTS.queue[idx] = 0 as i32;
    SLOTS.dir[idx] = 0 as usize;
    SLOTS.ev[idx] = 0 as usize;
    SLOTS.ovl[idx] = 0 as usize;
    SLOTS.buf[idx] = 0 as usize;
    SLOTS.vol[idx] = 0 as u32;
    SLOTS.ident[idx] = 0 as usize;
    return;
}

// ---- which directory a target needs watched ----------------------------------

// The parent of `path`, or "." when it names no directory at all. Both
// separators are honoured: Win32 accepts `/` everywhere and this package's own
// tests use it.
fn _parent_of(path: str) -> text::Text {
    let n: usize = #str_len(path);
    let b: *u8 = #str_ptr(path);
    var cut: isize = (0 as isize) -% (1 as isize);
    var i: usize = 0 as usize;
    while i < n {
        let c: u8 = { *(b + i) };
        if c == (47 as u8) || c == (92 as u8) { cut = i as isize; }
        i = i +% (1 as usize);
    }
    if cut < (0 as isize) { return text::from_str("."); }
    // A separator at index 0 means the root itself — `/x.txt`.
    if cut == (0 as isize) { return text::from_str("/"); }
    return text::from_str({ #str_from_raw_parts(b, cut as usize) });
}

// A directory's identity: volume serial (offset 28) plus the 64-bit file index.
// This is the same pair `metadata` reports as an inode, and it is what lets two
// spellings of one directory be recognised as one watch.
struct DirId { vol: u32, idx: usize, ok: bool }

fn _dir_id(h: *u8) -> DirId {
    let none: DirId = DirId { vol: 0 as u32, idx: 0 as usize, ok: false };
    let info: *u8 = { malloc(_bhfi_bytes()) };
    if info == (0 as *u8) { return none; }
    let rc: i32 = { GetFileInformationByHandle(h, info) };
    if rc == (0 as i32) { free(info); return none; }
    let vol: u32 = _read_u32(info, 28 as usize);
    let hi: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_index_high());
    let lo: u32 = _read_u32(info, _off_index_low());
    free(info);
    // 1<<32 built arithmetically: an integer literal wraps through i32 first.
    let shift: usize = (65536 as usize) *% (65536 as usize);
    return DirId { vol: vol, idx: ((hi as usize) *% shift) +% (lo as usize), ok: true };
}

// Is some slot in `queue` already watching this directory?
fn _already_watched(queue: i32, id: DirId) -> bool {
    if !id.ok { return false; }
    var i: usize = 0 as usize;
    while i < MAX_WATCHES {
        if SLOTS.queue[i] == queue && SLOTS.dir[i] != (0 as usize)
            && SLOTS.vol[i] == id.vol && SLOTS.ident[i] == id.idx {
            return true;
        }
        i = i +% (1 as usize);
    }
    return false;
}

// Close the QUEUE: every watch still open under it goes with it. The other
// backends get this for free because the queue is an fd the kernel owns; here
// the slots have to be walked.
fn fd_close(fd: i32) {
    if fd <= (0 as i32) { return; }
    var i: usize = 0 as usize;
    while i < MAX_WATCHES {
        if SLOTS.queue[i] == fd { _release(i); }
        i = i +% (1 as usize);
    }
    return;
}

// Release ONE watch. `queue` is unused here — the slot already knows which
// queue it belongs to — but it is in the signature because inotify needs it.
fn watch_close(queue: i32, handle: i32) {
    if handle < (0 as i32) { return; }
    let idx: usize = handle as usize;
    if idx >= MAX_WATCHES { return; }
    if SLOTS.queue[idx] == (0 as i32) { return; }
    _release(idx);
    return;
}

// Open the directory for change notifications and issue the first read.
// Returns the slot index, or -1 on failure.
fn _is_directory(path: str) -> bool {
    return match metadata(path) {
        MetadataResult::Found(m) => m.kind == NodeKind::Directory,
        _ => false,
    };
}

fn watch_path(queue: i32, path: str) -> i32 {
    if queue <= (0 as i32) { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    let slot: isize = _free_slot();
    if slot < (0 as isize) { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    let idx: usize = slot as usize;

    // WHICH DIRECTORY THIS TARGET NEEDS WATCHED. `ReadDirectoryChangesW` takes
    // only directories — opening a file with `FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY` to hand it
    // one fails with ACCESS_DENIED — while kqueue watches any vnode and inotify
    // any path, so the engine registers a watch for EVERY snapshot entry, files
    // included. A file target therefore resolves to its PARENT.
    //
    // A FILE AS THE WATCH ROOT is why this is not simply a passive slot. That is
    // what the file branch here used to be, on the reasoning that the wakeup
    // "ALREADY ARRIVES" from the watch on the containing directory — true for a
    // file the engine found while walking a directory root, and false for the
    // one case where the file IS the root: nothing else is watched, so nothing
    // ever signalled and `Watcher::new("a.txt")` never reported a single change.
    // Its own test caught it and it read as a package-wide failure.
    //
    // The passive slot is still right whenever the parent is ALREADY being
    // watched, and that is now decided by asking rather than by assuming — a
    // directory root with a hundred files keeps one handle, because each of
    // those files resolves to a parent this queue already holds. Without the
    // check the same hundred files would open a hundred handles on one directory
    // and exhaust MAX_WATCHES.
    let is_dir: bool = _is_directory(path);
    let parent: text::Text = _parent_of(path);
    let target: str = if is_dir { path } else { parent.view() };

    let wide: *u8 = _widen(target);
    if wide == (0 as *u8) { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    let dir: *u8 = { CreateFileW(wide, _file_list_directory(), _share_all(),
                                 0 as *u8, _open_existing(),
                                 _flag_backup_semantics() | _flag_overlapped(),
                                 0 as *u8) };
    free(wide);
    // A parent that cannot be opened is not fatal for a FILE target: the engine
    // only needs a wakeup, and refusing here would fail the whole watch over a
    // directory it never asked to watch. A directory target that cannot be
    // opened is the caller's own path and does fail.
    if _is_bad_handle(dir) {
        if is_dir { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
        SLOTS.queue[idx] = queue;
        return idx as i32;
    }

    // Already covered by another slot in this queue: take a passive slot and
    // give the handle straight back.
    let id: DirId = _dir_id(dir);
    if _already_watched(queue, id) {
        let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(dir) };
        SLOTS.queue[idx] = queue;
        return idx as i32;
    }

    // Manual reset: `poll_one` decides when the event is consumed, and an
    // auto-reset event would be cleared by the zero-timeout wait itself.
    let ev: *u8 = { CreateEventW(0 as *u8, 1 as i32, 0 as i32, 0 as *u8) };
    if _is_bad_handle(ev) { let _c: i32 = { CloseHandle(dir) }; return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    let ovl: *u8 = { malloc(_overlapped_bytes()) };
    let buf: *u8 = { malloc(_notify_buf_bytes()) };
    if ovl == (0 as *u8) || buf == (0 as *u8) {
        if ovl != (0 as *u8) { free(ovl); }
        if buf != (0 as *u8) { free(buf); }
        let _ce: i32 = { CloseHandle(ev) };
        let _cd: i32 = { CloseHandle(dir) };
        return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32);
    }

    SLOTS.queue[idx] = queue;
    SLOTS.dir[idx] = { #addr(dir) };
    SLOTS.ev[idx] = { #addr(ev) };
    SLOTS.ovl[idx] = { #addr(ovl) };
    SLOTS.buf[idx] = { #addr(buf) };
    SLOTS.vol[idx] = id.vol;
    SLOTS.ident[idx] = id.idx;

    if !_arm(idx) { _release(idx); return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    return idx as i32;
}

// Non-blocking single-event drain. 1 = event, 0 = empty, -1 = error,
// -2 = overflow / rescan required.
//
// The notify records themselves are never read: the engine above rescans and
// diffs, so all this owes it is "something under this queue changed". That is
// also why a zero-byte completion (the overflow signal, where the kernel
// dropped records) is reported as -2 rather than being treated as spurious.
fn poll_one(queue: i32) -> i32 {
    if queue <= (0 as i32) { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
    var i: usize = 0 as usize;
    while i < MAX_WATCHES {
        // A passive slot (a file target — see `watch_path`) holds no event and
        // is never the source of a wakeup.
        if SLOTS.queue[i] == queue && SLOTS.ev[i] != (0 as usize) {
            let ev: *u8 = { SLOTS.ev[i] as *u8 };
            let waited: u32 = { WaitForSingleObject(ev, 0 as u32) };
            if waited == _wait_object_0() {
                let dir: *u8 = { SLOTS.dir[i] as *u8 };
                let ovl: *u8 = { SLOTS.ovl[i] as *u8 };
                var moved: u32 = 0 as u32;
                let ok: i32 = { GetOverlappedResult(dir, ovl, #addr_of(moved), 0 as i32) };
                let _r: i32 = { ResetEvent(ev) };
                let rearmed: bool = _arm(i);
                if ok == (0 as i32) { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
                if !rearmed { return (0 as i32) -% (1 as i32); }
                // Zero bytes transferred is the documented overflow report.
                if moved == (0 as u32) { return (0 as i32) -% (2 as i32); }
                return 1 as i32;
            }
        }
        i = i +% (1 as usize);
    }
    return 0 as i32;
}

// ---- tests ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// AGAINST THE REAL FILESYSTEM AND THE REAL VOLUME, because the whole point of
// the USN is a property of NTFS that no fake can stand in for. A machine whose
// scratch volume has no journal takes the `version == 0` branch of every test
// below and still exercises the code path — which is the case that must degrade
// to the old mtime-only behaviour rather than break.

fn _t_write(path: str, byte: u8, count: usize) {
    match fs::create(path) {
        result::Result[fs::File, result::IoError]::Ok(f) => {
            var file: fs::File = f;
            var bytes: vec::Vec[u8] = vec::new::[u8]();
            var i: usize = 0 as usize;
            while i < count { let _a = bytes.append(byte); i = i +% (1 as usize); }
            let _w = file.write_all(bytes);
            file.close();
        }
        result::Result[fs::File, result::IoError]::Err(_e) => { assert false; }
    }
    return;
}

fn _t_meta(path: str) -> Metadata {
    match metadata(path) {
        MetadataResult::Found(m) => { return m; }
        MetadataResult::Missing => { assert false; }
        MetadataResult::PermissionDenied => { assert false; }
        MetadataResult::InvalidPath => { assert false; }
        MetadataResult::Failed => { assert false; }
    }
    return Metadata { kind: NodeKind::Other, inode: 0 as u64, size: 0 as i64,
                      mtime_sec: 0 as i64, mtime_nsec: 0 as i64, mode: 0 as u16,
                      version: 0 as u64 };
}

#[test]
fn the_usn_moves_on_a_rewrite_the_clock_cannot_see() {
    // THE BUG THIS FIELD EXISTS FOR, reproduced. `LastWriteTime` advances about
    // every 13ms, so two writes of the same size inside one tick leave size and
    // mtime byte-identical and the snapshot differ sees no change at all.
    //
    // What is asserted is the INVARIANT, not the collision: the USN must move on
    // every rewrite. That makes the test independent of whether this particular
    // run happens to straddle a tick boundary — which is a race, and racing it
    // would make the suite flaky for no extra coverage.
    let path: str = "/tmp/cplus_fswatch_usn/f.bin";
    let _mk = fs::create_dir_all("/tmp/cplus_fswatch_usn");
    let _r0 = fs::remove_file(path);

    _t_write(path, 65 as u8, 4 as usize);
    let a: Metadata = _t_meta(path);
    _t_write(path, 66 as u8, 4 as usize);
    let b: Metadata = _t_meta(path);

    // Same file, same length — the two facts that make mtime the only signal.
    assert a.inode == b.inode;
    assert a.size == b.size;

    if a.version == (0 as u64) {
        // No journal on this volume. The field is then a constant that changes
        // nothing, which is exactly the documented fallback — and the OLD
        // behaviour is all that is left, so there is nothing more to assert.
        assert b.version == (0 as u64);
    } else {
        // The number that survives a tick collision.
        assert b.version != a.version;
        // And it only ever goes forward, which is what makes it usable as a
        // version at all rather than merely as a hash.
        assert b.version > a.version;
    }

    let _r1 = fs::remove_file(path);
    return;
}

#[test]
fn a_handle_with_no_usn_answers_zero_rather_than_failing() {
    // THE ERROR POLICY IS THE WHOLE SAFETY. A non-NTFS volume answers
    // ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION and a disabled journal answers zeroes; both have to
    // come back as 0, because 0 compares equal every time and so restores the
    // old mtime-only behaviour. Anything else would report a change on every
    // poll for every file on a USB stick.
    assert _usn_of(0 as *u8) == (0 as u64);
    let invalid: *u8 = ((0 as usize) -% (1 as usize)) as *u8;   // INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE
    assert _usn_of(invalid) == (0 as u64);
    return;
}

#[test]
fn the_usn_control_code_is_the_one_the_macro_folds_to() {
    // CTL_CODE(FILE_DEVICE_FILE_SYSTEM 9, 58, METHOD_NEITHER 3, FILE_ANY_ACCESS 0).
    // A wrong code is not a crash — it is a DIFFERENT filesystem control, which
    // is why it is folded from its parts here and pinned against the literal.
    assert _fsctl_read_file_usn_data() == (0x900EB as u32);
    // USN_RECORD_V2 puts the number after two u32-sized version words and two
    // 64-bit file references.
    assert _off_usn() == (24 as usize);
    return;
}
